From fdd3c851c0c32cf786c98a46b3c6ebfad6b00a6a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Xander Burchartz <210020462+xanderburchartz@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 12:50:09 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 09/13] iOS: statically linked plugins

iOS apps cannot load plugins at runtime. The iOS app links its plugins
statically and offers them to the plugin loader as if they were files in
the system plugin directory, so the rest of the loader works unchanged.
Built-in plugins count as system plugins (not removed as orphans), keep
their version without metadata, and get their Options page after the first
enable. The plugin list is shown as iOS cards.

pluginmanager.cpp also renames PanelHardBreakWrapper to
PluginPanelHardBreakWrapper. notification_manager_gui.cpp has a class with
the same name; upstream both are identical, but the iOS changes make them
differ, which would be an ODR violation.

The iOS implementation lives in the iOS app layer (app/ios/*_ios.cpp in the
opencpn-ios repository); the core only declares and calls it.

Apart from that rename, all changes are inside #ifdef __OCPN__IOS__.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
